#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Start and stop the simulation. One command each.

    ./tools/simctl start                 # full stack + RViz + patrol, 2D backend
    ./tools/simctl start --backend isaac # the same stack on Isaac physics, with a window
    ./tools/simctl stop                  # stop the robots, then tear down
    ./tools/simctl status                # what is running, and where
    ./tools/simctl teleop                # drive it yourself: Yahboom's keyboard keys
    ./tools/simctl patrol                # drive an already-running simulation
    ./tools/simctl estop --domain 20     # zero a named domain, explicitly

WHICH BACKEND YOU GET, AND WHY `start` ALONE IS NOT ISAAC
---------------------------------------------------------
`--backend` defaults to `2d`, so plain `simctl start` has never launched Isaac. That is
deliberate: the 2D robot starts in ~40 s, needs no GPU, has no physics at all, and is
what the whole stack was developed against. Isaac takes ~90 s, wants the GPU, and gives
you real contact physics, collisions and a window to watch.

Both replace only the FIRMWARE. Everything above -- bringup, the EKF, the governor, SLAM,
Nav2, RViz -- is identical and cannot tell them apart.

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
Starting the stack by hand is six commands with waits between them. Stopping it by hand is
worse, because the obvious way is wrong in a way that bites:

  * `pkill` sends SIGTERM, which killed a patrol process before its cleanup could publish
    a zero. The firmware retains commands indefinitely -- measured, and the simulator
    models it -- so the robot kept going and ended up 12 m outside a 4 m room.
  * Killing ROS nodes leaves stale /dev/shm/fastrtps* segments behind, and those silently
    block new DDS participants: nodes simply fail to appear, with no error. 410 of them
    accumulated in one session.
  * The ros2 daemon then caches a stale node list, reporting nodes that are gone and
    missing nodes that are running.
  * RViz instances pile up, and the old ones show frozen data.

THE ORDERING RULE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF `stop`
-----------------------------------------------------
STOP THE ROBOT BEFORE KILLING WHAT IS DRIVING IT. Every other order leaves a latched
command running on a robot with no watchdog. This is not a tidiness preference; it is the
difference between a stopped robot and one crossing the room.

WHAT `stop` DOES TO THE REAL CAR
--------------------------------
It publishes ZEROS to the car's domain, and nothing else. It does not kill the micro-ROS
agent or any hardware node. Stopping a robot is the fail-safe direction; killing its agent
is not -- that container runs with --rm, so stopping it deletes it and the link is lost.
"""
